1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,553
Does a 500 rupee a month SIP really build anything?

2
00:00:03,553 --> 00:00:06,136
This is the question Bhai asked me last week.

3
00:00:06,136 --> 00:00:08,720
The SIP app was open on his phone.

4
00:00:08,720 --> 00:00:12,272
On the screen, the money was right where it started. And he said, bhaiya,

5
00:00:12,272 --> 00:00:14,210
I'm going to stop it.

6
00:00:14,210 --> 00:00:17,395
Bhai started this SIP in June 2024.

7
00:00:17,395 --> 00:00:20,898
Every month, 500 rupees got deducted from the bank automatically.

8
00:00:20,898 --> 00:00:23,446
He thought, it's a small amount, it won't hurt.

9
00:00:23,446 --> 00:00:26,949
And slowly something big will build up. Everyone thinks this way,

10
00:00:26,949 --> 00:00:28,860
and this thinking isn't wrong.

11
00:00:28,860 --> 00:00:32,553
At dinner, Papa took out the FD receipts. He said,

12
00:00:32,553 --> 00:00:35,137
I told you, FD is safe.

13
00:00:35,137 --> 00:00:37,353
In the share market, money gets lost.

14
00:00:37,353 --> 00:00:39,938
Mummy was quietly listening, holding her tea.

15
00:00:39,938 --> 00:00:43,630
On her face was the same old worry: what if the money is gone?

16
00:00:43,630 --> 00:00:47,023
Behen looked up from her phone and asked directly: Bhai,

17
00:00:47,023 --> 00:00:49,490
do you even know what a SIP is?

18
00:00:49,490 --> 00:00:52,574
Or did you just hear it in some video and start?

19
00:00:52,574 --> 00:00:54,733
Bhai went quiet. The truth is,

20
00:00:54,733 --> 00:00:57,200
most people start a SIP without understanding it.

21
00:00:57,200 --> 00:00:57,897
So let's get started,

22
00:00:57,897 --> 00:01:01,384
let's talk about that same dinner table today.

23
00:01:01,384 --> 00:01:03,127
Today there are three questions. First,

24
00:01:03,127 --> 00:01:06,963
how does a 500 rupee SIP actually work? Then,

25
00:01:06,963 --> 00:01:10,798
what happens if the market doesn't go straight up? And finally,

26
00:01:10,798 --> 00:01:12,890
who quietly eats up your money?

27
00:01:12,890 --> 00:01:15,480
First, the simple thing: what is a SIP.

28
00:01:15,480 --> 00:01:19,550
SIP means Systematic Investment Plan. It means that every month on a fixed date,

29
00:01:19,550 --> 00:01:22,140
a fixed amount goes into a mutual fund.

30
00:01:22,140 --> 00:01:25,840
A mutual fund means the money of many people in one place.

31
00:01:25,840 --> 00:01:29,540
A professional fund manager invests it in shares or bonds.

32
00:01:29,540 --> 00:01:33,471
And the best part is, you don't have to start with a big amount.

33
00:01:33,471 --> 00:01:38,117
According to AMFI, a SIP can start from as little as 500 rupees a month.

34
00:01:38,117 --> 00:01:41,334
That's just skipping one pizza from outside in a month.

35
00:01:41,334 --> 00:01:44,550
You don't need to be rich, you need to start.

36
00:01:44,550 --> 00:01:47,994
But this is where people make the first mistake. They think,

37
00:01:47,994 --> 00:01:51,437
SIP is a scheme that gives a fixed return. No.

38
00:01:51,437 --> 00:01:53,848
SIP is just a way of putting money in.

39
00:01:53,848 --> 00:01:57,292
The return comes from the fund your money goes into.

40
00:01:57,292 --> 00:02:01,080
And the fund's return goes up and down with the market.

41
00:02:01,080 --> 00:02:05,349
Now the first question: how do these 500 rupees work?

42
00:02:05,349 --> 00:02:10,045
There's a heavy term for this: rupee cost averaging. Don't be scared,

43
00:02:10,045 --> 00:02:12,180
it means something very simple.

44
00:02:12,180 --> 00:02:15,168
Let's understand it through the vegetable market,

45
00:02:15,168 --> 00:02:17,730
where the wife goes every week.

46
00:02:17,730 --> 00:02:19,413
Suppose tomatoes are cheap,

47
00:02:19,413 --> 00:02:23,115
then you get more kilos for the same money. When tomatoes are expensive,

48
00:02:23,115 --> 00:02:24,462
you get less.

49
00:02:24,462 --> 00:02:27,828
If you buy tomatoes with the same amount of money every week,

50
00:02:27,828 --> 00:02:30,520
what will happen? Sometimes you'll get more, sometimes less.

51
00:02:30,520 --> 00:02:32,540
The average price will come out somewhere in the middle.

52
00:02:32,540 --> 00:02:34,677
The same thing happens in a mutual fund.

53
00:02:34,677 --> 00:02:37,731
The fund's price changes every day, it's called NAV.

54
00:02:37,731 --> 00:02:41,090
NAV means the price of one unit of the fund on that day.

55
00:02:41,090 --> 00:02:43,533
Whatever number of units your 500 rupees can buy,

56
00:02:43,533 --> 00:02:45,670
that many get added to your account.

57
00:02:45,670 --> 00:02:48,012
When the market is high, NAV is expensive,

58
00:02:48,012 --> 00:02:51,022
so you get fewer units. When the market is down,

59
00:02:51,022 --> 00:02:53,699
NAV is cheap, so you get more units.

60
00:02:53,699 --> 00:02:57,044
You don't have to do anything. The money is deducted from your bank,

61
00:02:57,044 --> 00:02:59,720
and the system averages it out on its own.

62
00:02:59,720 --> 00:03:01,450
Now look at Bhai's mistake.

63
00:03:01,450 --> 00:03:04,910
He only looked at the total value on the screen. The value hadn't gone up,

64
00:03:04,910 --> 00:03:06,986
so he thought he had made a loss.

65
00:03:06,986 --> 00:03:10,792
But he didn't see that in the falling market, his 500

66
00:03:10,792 --> 00:03:12,868
rupees were buying more units.

67
00:03:12,868 --> 00:03:15,290
Those very units are the real treasure for the future.

68
00:03:15,290 --> 00:03:16,240
That's why they say,

69
00:03:16,240 --> 00:03:19,406
for someone doing a SIP, a falling market is not an enemy.

70
00:03:19,406 --> 00:03:20,989
It's a sale season.

71
00:03:20,989 --> 00:03:24,471
When a shop has a sale, we run to it.

72
00:03:24,471 --> 00:03:28,270
When the share market has a sale, we run away.

73
00:03:28,270 --> 00:03:29,220
Isn't that backwards?

74
00:03:29,220 --> 00:03:31,595
But listen to one honest thing too.

75
00:03:31,595 --> 00:03:34,309
Rupee cost averaging doesn't make losses disappear.

76
00:03:34,309 --> 00:03:37,363
If the market stays down for a very long time,

77
00:03:37,363 --> 00:03:39,399
then even averaging can't save you.

78
00:03:39,399 --> 00:03:43,470
All it does is that you don't have to search for the right time.

79
00:03:43,470 --> 00:03:47,563
Nobody knows the right time for the market. Not the TV people,

80
00:03:47,563 --> 00:03:51,656
not the friends who give tips. This is called market timing,

81
00:03:51,656 --> 00:03:54,931
That means buy at the very bottom and sell at the very top.

82
00:03:54,931 --> 00:03:57,796
Even big players fail at this.

83
00:03:57,796 --> 00:04:01,480
SIP takes you out of this game.

84
00:04:01,480 --> 00:04:02,545
Now the second question,

85
00:04:02,545 --> 00:04:05,739
the one that was worrying Bhai the most.

86
00:04:05,739 --> 00:04:09,642
What do we do if the market doesn't go straight up and just keeps wandering around the same place?

87
00:04:09,642 --> 00:04:12,127
This question isn't only Bhai's.

88
00:04:12,127 --> 00:04:15,321
A lot of SIP investors are thinking the same thing today.

89
00:04:15,321 --> 00:04:17,450
First, let's see what happened.

90
00:04:17,450 --> 00:04:21,937
Value Research looked at the data of the Nifty Total Return Index.

91
00:04:21,937 --> 00:04:26,015
Total Return means the price plus the dividends added in.

92
00:04:26,015 --> 00:04:30,502
From June 2024 to June 2026, it gave only 2.2 percent.

93
00:04:30,502 --> 00:04:32,949
In such a long time, just this much.

94
00:04:32,949 --> 00:04:36,620
Bhai's entire SIP period passed in this stretch.

95
00:04:36,620 --> 00:04:39,159
And by the end of June in 2026,

96
00:04:39,159 --> 00:04:40,973
the index was 8.1 percent down.

97
00:04:40,973 --> 00:04:45,326
That means the market is almost standing where it was when Bhai started.

98
00:04:45,326 --> 00:04:47,866
On top of that, it fell even more this year.

99
00:04:47,866 --> 00:04:51,493
What Bhai saw on his screen was not a lie.

100
00:04:51,493 --> 00:04:53,670
The market really was tired.

101
00:04:53,670 --> 00:04:56,567
Such a time is called a flat or sideways market.

102
00:04:56,567 --> 00:04:59,463
That means the market neither goes up nor goes down much,

103
00:04:59,463 --> 00:05:03,003
it just moves around here and there. This is the most boring time.

104
00:05:03,003 --> 00:05:06,865
And it is in boring times that people stop their SIPs the most.

105
00:05:06,865 --> 00:05:09,440
The mind says, nothing is happening at all.

106
00:05:09,440 --> 00:05:11,793
Bhai's logic was the same. He said,

107
00:05:11,793 --> 00:05:14,735
when I'm not getting anything, why should I put in money?

108
00:05:14,735 --> 00:05:17,677
Better I put it in an FD, and Papa will be happy too.

109
00:05:17,677 --> 00:05:20,031
This logic sounds completely right when you hear it.

110
00:05:20,031 --> 00:05:22,090
But there is a hidden mistake in it.

111
00:05:22,090 --> 00:05:27,110
The mistake is that even in a flat market, your 500 rupees are not standing still.

112
00:05:27,110 --> 00:05:31,054
They are buying units, and more units when the price falls.

113
00:05:31,054 --> 00:05:33,564
If and when the market moves ahead,

114
00:05:33,564 --> 00:05:36,433
these cheap units are the ones that help the most.

115
00:05:36,433 --> 00:05:39,660
Those who stop don't have them at all.

116
00:05:39,660 --> 00:05:42,656
Now understand compounding. Compounding means interest on interest,

117
00:05:42,656 --> 00:05:44,154
or return on return.

118
00:05:44,154 --> 00:05:49,398
It works only when money stays invested for a long time without a break.

119
00:05:49,398 --> 00:05:52,768
If you stop the SIP, the chain of compounding is broken.

120
00:05:52,768 --> 00:05:55,390
And lost time never comes back.

121
00:05:55,390 --> 00:05:58,219
Imagine a boy goes to the gym every month,

122
00:05:58,219 --> 00:06:02,110
but his body doesn't build up. After a few months, he quits.

123
00:06:02,110 --> 00:06:04,939
And the very month his body would have started to show results,

124
00:06:04,939 --> 00:06:06,708
he isn't there.

125
00:06:06,708 --> 00:06:10,598
Stopping your SIP is just like that. You went through the hard days,

126
00:06:10,598 --> 00:06:12,720
and on the days of reward, you're gone.

127
00:06:12,720 --> 00:06:14,599
But here too, an honest word.

128
00:06:14,599 --> 00:06:17,606
Nobody can say when the market will take off.

129
00:06:17,606 --> 00:06:20,613
It could even stay flat for a longer time.

130
00:06:20,613 --> 00:06:25,123
That's why do SIP only with money you won't need soon.

131
00:06:25,123 --> 00:06:28,130
Then you won't have to sell in a falling market.

132
00:06:28,130 --> 00:06:31,832
Now the third question. Who is quietly eating your money?

133
00:06:31,832 --> 00:06:34,054
My sister asked exactly this. Brother,

134
00:06:34,054 --> 00:06:37,756
does a fund manager work for free? Not at all.

135
00:06:37,756 --> 00:06:40,348
His fee is called the expense ratio.

136
00:06:40,348 --> 00:06:42,940
And this fee is charged every year.

137
00:06:42,940 --> 00:06:45,911
Expense ratio means the yearly cost of running the fund.

138
00:06:45,911 --> 00:06:49,625
The fund manager's salary, office, research, all of it is included.

139
00:06:49,625 --> 00:06:52,596
It doesn't come as a separate line in your bill.

140
00:06:52,596 --> 00:06:55,939
It is quietly deducted from the fund's value.

141
00:06:55,939 --> 00:06:58,910
That's why most people never even look at it.

142
00:06:58,910 --> 00:06:59,936
Think of a kirana shop.

143
00:06:59,936 --> 00:07:03,015
The shopkeeper keeps a small margin on every item.

144
00:07:03,015 --> 00:07:05,067
On one item, you don't notice.

145
00:07:05,067 --> 00:07:07,119
But add up the whole year,

146
00:07:07,119 --> 00:07:09,171
and it comes to a good amount.

147
00:07:09,171 --> 00:07:12,250
Expense ratio is the same, it looks small,

148
00:07:12,250 --> 00:07:13,960
but it is charged every year.

149
00:07:13,960 --> 00:07:16,826
And it is charged on your entire saved money,

150
00:07:16,826 --> 00:07:18,737
not just on the new 500 rupees.

151
00:07:18,737 --> 00:07:20,648
As the corpus grows bigger,

152
00:07:20,648 --> 00:07:22,559
the fees also keep growing.

153
00:07:22,559 --> 00:07:25,425
Corpus means your total saved money, along with returns.

154
00:07:25,425 --> 00:07:28,610
Over a long time, this small difference becomes big.

155
00:07:28,610 --> 00:07:29,871
Now the useful part.

156
00:07:29,871 --> 00:07:32,708
Every fund has direct and regular plans.

157
00:07:32,708 --> 00:07:35,860
In a regular plan, there is an agent or distributor in between,

158
00:07:35,860 --> 00:07:37,751
and his commission gets added too.

159
00:07:37,751 --> 00:07:39,958
In a direct plan, there is no such commission.

160
00:07:39,958 --> 00:07:43,740
That's why a direct plan's expense ratio is usually lower.

161
00:07:43,740 --> 00:07:45,678
And there's one more hidden cost: tax.

162
00:07:45,678 --> 00:07:47,939
If you hold an equity fund for more than a year,

163
00:07:47,939 --> 00:07:50,847
the profit is called long term capital gain.

164
00:07:50,847 --> 00:07:52,785
12.5 percent tax is charged on it.

165
00:07:52,785 --> 00:07:57,630
But every year, such profit up to 1.25 lakh rupees is exempt from tax.

166
00:07:57,630 --> 00:08:02,287
For someone with a 500 rupee SIP, this tax will hardly apply in the beginning.

167
00:08:02,287 --> 00:08:05,153
But keep it in mind. When you look at returns, always think,

168
00:08:05,153 --> 00:08:08,377
what will be left in your hand after costs and tax.

169
00:08:08,377 --> 00:08:11,960
That is the real return. Everything else is just the shine of the screen.

170
00:08:11,960 --> 00:08:14,907
Now back to the dinner table. Brother asked,

171
00:08:14,907 --> 00:08:17,118
so is FD useless? No.

172
00:08:17,118 --> 00:08:20,802
Papa's FD has its own place. Emergency money,

173
00:08:20,802 --> 00:08:23,381
money you need quickly, it's fine there.

174
00:08:23,381 --> 00:08:28,170
Bank deposits also have insurance cover up to 5 lakh rupees.

175
00:08:28,170 --> 00:08:31,655
The biggest mistake is making decisions out of fear. The market falls,

176
00:08:31,655 --> 00:08:35,914
you see a red screen in the news, and the SIP stops. Then the market recovers,

177
00:08:35,914 --> 00:08:39,399
and people come back at the higher price.

178
00:08:39,399 --> 00:08:41,722
That means they left it when it was cheap and bought it when it was expensive.

179
00:08:41,722 --> 00:08:44,820
This is the thing that spoils SIP the most.

180
00:08:44,820 --> 00:08:48,742
Another mistake is picking a fund by looking only at its past return.

181
00:08:48,742 --> 00:08:51,237
The fund that was at the top last year

182
00:08:51,237 --> 00:08:55,159
may not stay there ahead. And one more mistake, opening the app every day.

183
00:08:55,159 --> 00:08:58,724
If you open it every day, you'll feel fear every day. SIP is a long race,

184
00:08:58,724 --> 00:09:00,150
not a daily score.

185
00:09:00,150 --> 00:09:02,887
So what should you do? First an emergency fund, then SIP.

186
00:09:02,887 --> 00:09:06,309
Keep the SIP at an amount that can continue even in a bad month,

187
00:09:06,309 --> 00:09:08,704
even if it is just 500 rupees.

188
00:09:08,704 --> 00:09:11,099
Check the direct plan and the expense ratio.

189
00:09:11,099 --> 00:09:14,520
And look at your accounts just a few times a year, not every day.

190
00:09:14,520 --> 00:09:17,551
And when your salary goes up, increase your SIP a little too.

191
00:09:17,551 --> 00:09:19,236
This is called a step-up SIP,

192
00:09:19,236 --> 00:09:22,604
meaning increasing the SIP amount every year. Brother said,

193
00:09:22,604 --> 00:09:24,625
Okay brother, I won't stop it.

194
00:09:24,625 --> 00:09:28,330
Papa kept the FD receipt and gave a faint smile.

195
00:09:28,330 --> 00:09:29,819
Let me make one thing clear.

196
00:09:29,819 --> 00:09:32,201
I am not naming any fund,

197
00:09:32,201 --> 00:09:34,584
and I am not promising any return.

198
00:09:34,584 --> 00:09:36,669
The market goes up, and it goes down too.

199
00:09:36,669 --> 00:09:39,349
This is not investment advice. Look at your own needs,

200
00:09:39,349 --> 00:09:40,540
and make your decision wisely.

201
00:09:40,540 --> 00:09:45,463
In the next video, we'll talk about PPF and EPF,

202
00:09:45,463 --> 00:09:48,745
which Papa likes so much.

203
00:09:48,745 --> 00:09:53,122
Are they really better than SIP?

204
00:09:53,122 --> 00:09:55,857
But before that, a question for you.

205
00:09:55,857 --> 00:10:00,780
Have you ever stopped your SIP when the market fell?

206
00:10:00,780 --> 00:10:04,062
Yes or no, write in the comments.
